{
  "image_name": "000a2ea691cf0656642ea78b098b46a8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how SARS‑CoV‑2 may infect the intestinal lining and trigger local and systemic inflammation. It contrasts an uninfected gut epithelium with an infected one, centering on an enterocyte or goblet cell that expresses ACE2 and the protease TMPRSS2. The virus binds at the apical surface, enters the cell, and then replicates inside, where genomic RNA is copied through double‑membrane vesicles and subgenomic RNAs, with the ER and Golgi involved in assembly and release of new viral particles.\n\nA second major theme is the host response. Viral RNA and proteins appear to activate pattern‑recognition pathways, leading to interferons, cytokines, and inflammasome signaling. Pro‑IL‑18 is processed to IL‑18, suggesting inflammatory amplification. Immune cells in the gut tissue, including dendritic cells, neutrophils, macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells, are shown as becoming involved, with mediators such as IL‑6, IL‑10, IL‑2, TNF, calprotectin, and SARS‑CoV‑2–specific IgA indicated nearby. These responses appear linked to broader inflammatory consequences, possibly contributing to cytokine release syndrome. The figure also highlights microbiota alteration during infection, with reduced diversity and increased opportunistic pathogens.",
